WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:06.450
JEFF SISSON: Hi.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.240
My name's Jeff Sisson, and I
work at "The New York Times"

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.050
as part of a team that builds
the tools the newsroom uses

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.970
to publish our journalism.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.920
I hope to describe
how our team uses

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.160
open source technologies
and Firestore to create

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.860
a completely unique rich
text editor for "The New York

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.910
Times."

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.820
But first, I want to
describe a radical position

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.220
I've come to believe as
a byproduct of our work

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.230
on tools for the newsroom.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.490
Hear me out.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:37.210
Everyone deserves their own
collaborative rich text editor.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.440
So let me explain
what I mean here.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.240
Rich text editors,
like Google Docs,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.000
have made what you see is
what you get style editing

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.545
a seamless experience.

00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:49.570
This actually makes it
harder to appreciate

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.540
some of the complexity that
underlies a pleasant rich text

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.440
editor.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.210
Most people just
want it to work.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.980
They want to work
in an editor that

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.830
produces HTML-like
documents, which

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.030
may include bold, italics,
strikethrough, other emphasis.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.260
And they want to compose using
paragraph, lists, possibly even

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.620
tables or images or other
more complex layouts.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.480
Even when just
editing plain text,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.630
there are lots of subtleties
that editors like Google Docs

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.400
or Word handle for you.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.235
There's live-as-you-type
text suggestions for grammar

00:01:21.235 --> 00:01:25.120
or spelling errors or the
ability for others to comment

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.330
on the document or tracking
the changes that you've made

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.310
to a document over time.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.140
And it's now even
a common assumption

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.600
that if you're editing a rich
text document in a browser,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.730
it must be networked,
meaning anyone

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.530
can view the changes
as you're making them

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.800
and contribute their
own changes live.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.990
So all of this is
obviously a lot.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.180
It's reasonable to
wonder why anyone

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.460
would want to create their
own rich text editor that

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.010
does all of these things
rather than relying

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.890
on one of the many capable
text editors that exist,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:01.380
like Google Docs or Word or even
tools like Medium or WordPress.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.830
A little bit more detail on our
work at "The New York Times"

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.400
where the tools that we
maintain for editors, producers,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.450
and reporters facilitate
publishing articles

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.250
and our coverage of the news.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.590
So "The New York Times"
has a huge newsroom.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.960
At any time of day,
someone is working

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.030
to report on world
events in articles

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.190
that are often published while
that news is still happening.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.590
We've had a number of
different CMS's over the years.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.370
And when we set out to
build a new article editor,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.100
we knew that we wanted it
to be collaborative first.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.710
Photo editors should be
able to place photos,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.750
while other editors
are polishing a draft

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.790
of the text ready to publish.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.560
And, yet, other
editors may be readying

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.680
new quotes or new information
for later publishing.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.270
And all of this
needs to be happening

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.750
for users that are located in
bureaus around the globe split

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.430
amongst many
different time zones.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.360
Enter Oak.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.260
So what is Oak?

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.960
Oak is the name of
the rich text editor

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.000
we built that
powers every article

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.460
published by "The
New York Times."

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.650
What are we looking
at in this screenshot?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.700
In the white canvas
in the center,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.050
you can see that the basic
skeleton of an empty "New York

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.970
Times" article exists.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:16.830
You have a headline, a summary,
some bylines, and a timestamp.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.780
Below that, we have
placeholder texts

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.560
that commands you to
begin writing where

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.000
the innards of the article go.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.190
So things like paragraphs,
images, videos,

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.480
interactive graphics,
div text, scrolling text,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.640
and image combinations,
contributor bylines and much,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:34.680
much more.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.620
This is the basic canvas
for Oak's rich text blocks.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.840
And to the left of that canvas,
there are extra textual tools,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.740
like Spell Check, and validation
for "New York Times'" specific

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.010
writing rules, and a
space for writing drafts.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.298
All of these things
together are workflow tools

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:55.590
that are specifically tuned
to how "The New York Times"

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.910
newsroom works.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.950
And very few of
these features exist

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.060
in other off-the-rack
rich text editors.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.130
So why did we build
Oak from scratch

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.840
rather than building
something on top

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.630
of a different
proprietary editor?

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.510
"The New York Times" has a
specific editorial process

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.850
that is very unique to itself.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.560
We use a style
guide that governs

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.760
how articles are written.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.240
There are rules for how
obituary headlines are written--

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.380
rules for the use of courtesy
titles such as Mr. and Ms.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.290
And rules for the use of
date lines, like WASHINGTON,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.180
in all caps at the
top of an article

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.250
to indicate that a reporter
was actually working

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.260
from a specific location.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.020
These editorial nuances are
part of what contributes

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.180
to "The Times's" unique voice.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.650
And building our
own editor allowed

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.980
us to incorporate these rules
into how the editor works

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.410
so that the newsroom can
see feedback for an article

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.020
as it's being
written and edited.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.080
Before it's published,
reporters may

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.030
contribute a first
draft of an article,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.010
then hand off to
a first editor who

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.190
may work with the second
editor or copy editor.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.320
Some highly sensitive
articles are even

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.580
edited with a privacy mode so
that only parts of the newsroom

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.430
that are working on them
can see the articles prior

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:12.870
to publication.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.960
All of these rules for around
who can access the story

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.480
and edit it at different parts
of an article's editorial

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.440
lifecycle were only able
to be built into Oak

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.600
because we were building
an editor from scratch.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.920
And our editor also aims for
what you see is what you mean,

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:30.490
fidelity.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:31.610
And what does that mean?

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:37.260
So when editors are working
in the Oak article editor,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.540
all of the styles for headlines,
paragraphs, and other elements

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.360
that you see as a reader
are also shown to them,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.480
meaning that the newsroom has
instant feedback on exactly

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.030
how their story will
look when it's published.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.250
Many rich text editors
are focused primarily

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.240
on the editing.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.150
And so this ability to sort of
see the shape of the article

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.300
as it's being written has helped
to change the kinds of articles

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.160
that we publish.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.840
All of these
features together are

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.420
difficult to achieve in
off-the-rack rich text

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.550
editors, which is part
of why most of Oak

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.860
was built from scratch to
accommodate our newsroom's

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.070
needs.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.280
We want our reporters,
editors, and producers

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.680
to focus on
contributing journalism

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.750
and let the rich
text editor itself

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:19.390
do much of the heavy lifting
around editorial workflow

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.340
and document production.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.520
But we didn't attempt to
build our writer alone.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.170
The main technical
component that

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.390
allows "The New York
Times" and, of course, you

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.300
if you want to create the
rich text editor of our dreams

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.070
is called ProseMirror.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:36.280
It's a set of open source
JavaScript libraries developed

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.910
by [? Maryne ?] [? Haverbeck ?]
that do the hard parts of rich

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.490
text editing in web browsers.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.790
The browser technology that
most rich text editors rely on

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.010
is something called
content editable.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.990
And as a software
spec, it's actually

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.090
very messy and inconsistent.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.900
How input events, like pastes,
key presses, and selections

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:59.410
are interpreted vary wildly
between different browsers.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:02.320
And there are many long-standing
bugs and gotchas even

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:04.980
on modern browsers
like Google Chrome.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.730
ProseMirror
abstracts around many

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.070
of these inconsistencies,
which allows

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.710
developers to work with
straightforward primitives.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.420
You have a documents
that's represented

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:17.380
by a node, a selection that
uses a selection class.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.630
And changes to a
document are represented

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.850
by transforms and transactions.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.660
ProseMirror also handles
painting some of these updates

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.450
to a rich text
document into the DOM.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.420
When a document changes,
whether that document is changed

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.460
by text that you've
typed into it

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.800
or via a remote
collaborating peer,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.140
ProseMirror will make sure
the DOM reflects the latest

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:41.750
state of the document.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:44.360
In ProseMirror, everything
is a node, even the top level

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:45.440
document.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.890
Each top level document node
contains one or more children,

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.750
each of which may contain
their own children

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.520
and have attributes.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.580
This document here on screen
represents a very simple

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:01.950
paragraph with the text Hello
world bolded and italicized.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.780
But documents, of course,
can be much more complex.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.320
So every article in "The
New York Times" publishes

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.425
contains many types
of different nodes.

00:08:09.425 --> 00:08:10.800
And the purpose
of the Oak editor

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.450
is to allow editors
in the newsroom

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.930
the flexibility to produce
new combinations of nodes that

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.090
may share some of the
same node patterns

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:21.150
with other articles that may
use different types of nodes

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.430
or different orders
of nodes to emphasize

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.940
a particular structure of
a story within an article.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.650
Here, you can see
what our readers

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.110
would see for an article
that was created in Oak

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:35.960
and appears on our website
about pollution in China.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.010
And here's that same article
as it appears in the Oak editor

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:42.130
along with a view of the
node data structure that

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.200
underlies the article.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.860
All of the rules for what
types of document can contain

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.330
and their order
live in something

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.570
called a schema definition
that ProseMirror understands

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:56.295
and is able to enforce as you
make changes to the document.

00:08:56.295 --> 00:08:58.420
So whenever I'm reading a
"New York Times" article,

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.020
I often think about it and
its underlying node structure

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.990
and schema with a sort
of X-ray view in my head.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.540
This article about curbing
pollution starts with a header.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.950
And the header contains
itself headline, summary,

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.360
a leading media note,
and a byline node.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.340
And the header is
followed by a paragraph

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.340
that contains text
itself contains

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.600
a link, and so on and so forth.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.890
So ProseMirror schema
allows you to impose

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.660
really far-fetched constraints
on your document shape.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.980
It has its own language which
is a subset of RegEx that

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.290
can mandate complex
rules of adjacency

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.380
for different types of nodes
or constrain how many nodes

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.170
of a specific type can be
added in a specific position

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:40.550
or in a row.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.380
This fine-grained
control over what

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.170
is valid for a
rich text document

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.390
unlocks many fascinating
opportunities

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:50.330
for creating rich text
editors with experiences

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.390
that are tuned to different
kinds of documents

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:54.780
that they were built for.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:56.480
So just to take
some examples, you

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.840
might have a Haiku editor that
has live feedback as you're

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.390
typing about the number
of syllables per line

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.940
and constrains the
lines as you type them.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.130
Or an Oulipo editor
where two people

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:09.650
are working on the same
document at the same time

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.740
but only see a shared
strand of text between them

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:16.640
so they compose stories as
part of one holistic whole.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.210
Or maybe you'd have a
highly structured FAQ editor

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:23.300
that offers suggestions
about the types of headings

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:24.590
as you type them.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:28.160
Or an email editor that
intersperses emoji or color

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.360
as you're writing them.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.902
With ProseMirror,
the different kinds

00:10:30.902 --> 00:10:33.620
of schemas that you can produce
are really unlimited which

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:34.565
is extremely powerful.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.400
With a schema in place
for your document,

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:41.410
it's time to start editing.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.300
Every change to a
document, whether it's

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:46.570
typing text into a paragraph,
dragging or dropping an image,

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:50.350
or italicizing existing text
produces a data structure

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.620
in ProseMirror
that's called a step.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:56.170
The basic recipe for how you
go from one document state

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.940
to the next document state
is by adding a step to it.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:03.160
Steps are created from
an existing document

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.080
and referred to an
indexing system,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:07.870
whereby, each letter or
leaf node within a document

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.650
can be addressed with a number.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:12.970
So in our example, Hello
world doc, the H in Hello

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:14.470
is at index two.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:18.110
And similarly, the d in
world starts at index 12.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.680
So if I was to type
in exclamation point

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.570
into a ProseMirror
managed editor

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:25.600
with this document in
it, the exclamation point

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.850
would be represented as
a replace step, which

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:31.870
is a specific class of step
at position 12 containing

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:37.090
a string which contains
the exclamation point.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.820
ProseMirror's
collaborative plugin

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:41.650
tracks the local
state of the document

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.560
using a monotonic counter
that starts at zero.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.120
So if you have an
empty document,

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.865
you have a document with
a step counter of zero.

00:11:48.865 --> 00:11:51.160
For each step produced
in the local document,

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.410
the counter increments by one.

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:55.960
Once the step is created,
the ProseMirror collaborative

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.760
plugin requires you
to implement what's

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.730
called an authority server
in order for that step

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.440
to be synced remotely.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:04.390
The authority
server's job is either

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:08.228
to accept or reject incoming
steps from multiple clients

00:12:08.228 --> 00:12:10.270
according to whether those
clients are attempting

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.910
to insert steps in
or out of order.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:14.410
Clients whose steps
are acknowledged

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:17.530
by the authority's server
continue on their merry way.

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:19.990
Clients whose steps are rejected
because their steps were

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:23.110
submitted out of order and don't
reflect their other peers steps

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.330
need to perform an
operation that basically

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:27.850
operates like a git rebase.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.380
The ProseMirror
collaborative plugin

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:33.640
will take into account any steps
that the local client hasn't

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:39.770
yet fetched and change the steps
to reflect those other peers

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:40.930
as steps.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.150
Once this rebase
operation is complete,

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.820
the client can attempt to
resend their steps back

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:47.950
to the authority server.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:51.850
So while ProseMirror's
collaborative editing

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:54.790
architecture requires a central
authority server in order

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:58.960
to order incoming steps
from all connected clients,

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.272
it actually only provides
a reference implementation,

00:13:01.272 --> 00:13:02.980
which means you're on
the hook to provide

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.110
your own implementation.

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:08.320
This reference implementation
is only persisted in memory

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:10.220
and can't really scale.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.450
And so when we set out to build
collaborative editing in Oak,

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:16.090
we knew we needed to
build an authority server.

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:18.130
We evaluated several
different databases

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:21.190
and backend architectures to
support collaborative editing.

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:25.310
But ultimately we
settled on Firestore.

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.920
Part of Firestore's
appeal is that in addition

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.380
to providing traditional
database functionality,

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:33.080
so as it reads and
writes, it also

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:36.130
comes with a client-compatible
event bus built in.

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.090
For Oak, which is a
browser-based application,

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.030
this means that we're
able to read and write

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:43.670
changes from the database
directly in the browser.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:47.810
Oak uses redux and redux-saga
to manage its local state.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:50.540
And because Firestore
is an event bus as well,

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.210
we can treat Firestore as
if it was just another local

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.040
to the browser event sync.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:58.100
Steps arrived from Firestore
and wrapped in redux actions

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.110
that mutate the local state.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.120
This vastly simplified
the architecture

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.040
we needed to build our editor.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.220
We didn't need to build out
a CRUD API, an event bus.

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.990
And all the client-sided
[? after ?] code,

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:13.240
you need to integrate
with the custom back end.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.600
In the Oak editor, each
time a ProseMirror instance

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.870
produces a new step, we
serialize that step's data

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:22.210
to JSON and attempt
to write that step

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:25.330
to Firestore using the
latest known step ID plus 1

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.820
as the Firestore document key.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.760
For an empty document
starting at step ID 0,

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.500
we would attempt to
write our first step

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.540
with a Firestore key of one.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.310
And for the second
step, we would

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:40.380
attempt to write with a
Firestore key of two and so on.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:44.470
If a Firestore document exists
with the next most step ID key,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:46.085
we end the transaction.

00:14:46.085 --> 00:14:47.710
This signals to our
client code that we

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.830
need to rebase our local steps
to reflect steps we haven't yet

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.890
fetched from Firestore
before attempting to resend

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:55.460
our local steps again.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:58.450
But if the insert succeeds,
our Firestore listener

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:00.590
will see the steps
as they're inserted.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:03.010
And we will locally use
this to confirm them

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:06.290
so that subsequent steps
use the new document state

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:10.370
and step ID as the
starting point.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:12.200
When reading steps,
whether you're

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:15.260
confirming a step you
yourself inserted or receiving

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.150
a step from another
collaborator,

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:19.880
the implementation looks
exactly the same-- a Firestore

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:21.650
collection listener.

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.300
When the steps
collection is updated,

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:28.220
we fetch any steps greater than
the last step ID and apply them

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.590
to the local state
of the document.

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:32.540
Because the listener
executes whether or not

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:37.310
the steps you are attempting
to insert have been successful,

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:41.060
rebasing other client steps are
handled using the same listener

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.110
as well.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.720
The Firestore listener acts like
an event stream for any step

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.850
that's been confirmed
remotely by Firestore,

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:50.610
whether those steps are
your own or someone else's.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:55.440
And here, I have a
demo of that Oak editor

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:00.670
that's been modified to sort
of show this process in action.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:03.930
This is a simplified version
of the Oak article editor.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:07.045
Normally, Firestore's APIs
are nearly instantaneous.

00:16:07.045 --> 00:16:08.920
So I've changed it so
that calls to Firestore

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:11.520
are artificially slower than
they would be in real life

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:13.900
to make it easier to
see what's going on.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:16.200
I've also added this
text at the top here

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:18.930
that illustrates communication
with Firestore under the hood.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.960
And I've hidden other
parts of the app as well--

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.848
set up two tabs-- one
on the left and one

00:16:23.848 --> 00:16:25.890
on the right-- that are
connected to the same Oak

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:29.290
article that was just created,
meaning the step counter here

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:31.860
starts at zero.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.960
When I type the word hello in
the headline in the left tab,

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:37.830
a redux saga detects
that there are

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.660
steps that haven't
been sent and attempts

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:41.580
to send them with a
Firestore transaction--

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.800
one for each letter.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:45.557
Once the Firestore
transaction completes,

00:16:45.557 --> 00:16:47.640
the Firestore collection
listener in the right tab

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:50.730
detects the latest steps and
applies them to the document,

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:54.620
bringing the steps collection
up to five steps stored.

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:57.545
Because this editor has been
artificially slowed down,

00:16:57.545 --> 00:16:58.920
we can actually
simulate the case

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:01.230
where both tabs attempt
to execute a Firestore

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.480
transaction at the same time.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.930
I'll type the word
world in the left tab

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.660
and hi in the right tab.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.940
The left will win, which
causes the right tab

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.420
to detect a conflict
and rebase its steps.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.390
After that, it
re-attempts a transaction

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.550
inserting its steps,
bringing both tabs up

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.900
to sync with each other.

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.859
Oak articles, of course, are
not just limited to text.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.680
Because every change is a
step, even pasting something

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.620
like a link to an
embedded tweet is

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.750
modeled as a step
that will be sent to

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.405
and stored in Firestore
in sync with both tabs.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.280
So ProseMirror itself
doesn't mandate

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.530
how collaborative
editing is implemented.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:46.000
And you're free to
choose whichever

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.060
backend suits your needs.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:50.710
Firestore was a good fit
for "The New York Times"

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:53.238
because we needed to build a
collaborative editor really

00:17:53.238 --> 00:17:55.030
quickly without worrying
too much about how

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.310
the backend would scale.

00:17:57.310 --> 00:17:59.410
While our daily number of
articles and production

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:02.680
is relatively modest, the true
scale for our collaborative app

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:05.630
is determined by the rate at
which most people can type,

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.270
which can add up quickly.

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.460
Choosing Firestore allowed
us to not worry about

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:10.835
whether or not
our database would

00:18:10.835 --> 00:18:13.500
handle our anticipated load.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.260
I think Firestore is also a
good fit for anyone building

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.420
collaborative editing
that's part of a broader

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:20.740
client-side heavy browser app.

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.730
It's really freeing to think of
Firestore as just another event

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:25.590
source within your
browser-based app

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.150
where mutations
to your app state

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:30.420
may come from remote
changes or local changes.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.550
And you can focus
your client code

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:34.530
on what those mutations
are doing rather

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:36.430
than how they're produced.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:39.090
Finally, Firestore
allowed us to simplify

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:41.080
our overall architecture.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:43.260
We didn't need to
build out a CRED API

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.660
or worry about backend
race conditions.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:50.870
We were mostly able to focus
on our apps business logic.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:54.290
I hope everyone is sufficiently
enthralled and inspired

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:57.890
to build a million tiny,
beautiful, collaborative rich

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:00.050
text editors for
whatever purposes

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:01.940
shoot your [? windsor ?] fancy.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:05.270
I want to give a shout out to
the Oak and publishing teams

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:06.530
at "The New York Times."

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:09.860
Have to say that the project of
building a collaborative editor

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:12.200
was truly a multi-year
collaborative

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:13.580
effort in its own right.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:16.590
And I'm proud of the work
we all jammed on together.

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:21.070
Thank you and go build the rich
text editor of your dreams.

